      </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Ranked Them</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills. The ranking is built from ProForm's published
            specifications and warranty terms, independent review coverage and, where one existed,
            a US retailer listing, all checked on 13 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started by confirming what ProForm actually sells. Its US treadmill category listed
            two series and four models, and each series page carried a side-by-side specification
            table we could read directly, alongside the individual product pages. We then compared
            every figure with independent reviews of the same model. Where ProForm and an
            independent source agreed, the figure went into the spec line. Where they did not, or
            where ProForm's own pages disagreed with each other, it stayed out.
          </p>
          <p>
            That second case came up more than we expected. The Carbon Series product pages carry a
            one-year motor warranty badge while the series comparison table beneath them says two
            years. The Carbon Pro 2000's specification table gives a 3.0 CHP motor while its feature
            copy describes a 3.6 CHP one. These are almost certainly copy errors rather than two
            different machines, but a buyer cannot know which number is the error, so neither went
            on the card.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ranking itself weighs four things in order: the deck, because a 55-inch belt and a
            60-inch belt suit different users; the user capacity, because it is ProForm's own
            statement of how much load the frame and motor are designed for; the price at which
            each model is actually being sold rather than its list price; and the screen, last,
            because it is the component with the shortest warranty and the one most dependent on a
            paid membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="series-compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Pro Series vs Carbon Series</h2>
<p>
            ProForm's two series are split by one decision: whether the screen is built into the
            treadmill or brought by you. Almost everything else follows from that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pro Series: the built-in screen tier.</strong> The Carbon Pro 2000 and <a href="/proform-carbon-pro-9000-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Carbon
            Pro 9000</a> share a 20 by 60-inch belt, 0 to 12% incline with no decline, a 12 mph top
            speed, a 350 lb user capacity, a 7.75-inch step-up height and a folding frame. ProForm
            lists an in-box weight of about 249 lb for both. The difference is the screen and the
            motor: a 10-inch HD touchscreen on the 2000 and a 16-inch one on the 9000, with
            ProForm rating the 9000's motor at 3.6 CHP. Both carry ten years on the frame, two years
            on the motor and parts, and one year on the tablet and labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Carbon Series: the bring-your-own-device tier.</strong> The Carbon TLX and <a href="/proform-carbon-tls-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Carbon TLS</a> have small LCD displays — 7 inches and 5 inches — with a shelf for your own
            phone or tablet. You connect that device to the treadmill over Bluetooth, and iFIT then
            runs on the device and controls the machine's speed and incline. Both are rated to 300
            lb, both fold, and both reach 12% incline. They differ on the deck and the speed: the
            TLX has a 20 by 60-inch belt and 12 mph, the TLS an 18 by 55-inch belt and 10 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the series name does not tell you.</strong> Both series have the same
            incline ceiling, and neither offers decline. If you want to walk steep grades, nothing
            ProForm currently sells goes beyond 12%; NordicTrack's incline trainers reach 40%, and
            our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack roundup</a> covers them. And none of the four has a lifetime motor warranty, which is the clearest
            difference between ProForm and its sister brand's better machines; our <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack vs ProForm comparison</a> sets out where else the two diverge.
          </p>
          <p>
            A rough rule: walk mostly and want to spend least, choose the Carbon TLS; run, and
            want a screen, choose the Carbon Pro 2000; want the biggest screen, choose the Carbon
            Pro 9000; run, and already own a tablet you like, consider the Carbon TLX — but only
            when it is priced clearly below the Pro 2000.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Deck Size and User Capacity: The Figures That Decide It</h2>
<p>
            The screen gets the attention in ProForm's marketing, but the two figures that decide
            whether a ProForm suits you are the belt size and the user capacity, and both are
            confirmed for all four models.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt length.</strong> Three of the four have a 60-inch belt, which is the
            length generally recommended for running because a running stride is longer than a
            walking one and the belt has to accommodate it with room to spare. The Carbon TLS has a
            55-inch belt, which is comfortable for walking and gentle jogging but short for a full
            running stride, particularly for taller users. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide for tall runners</a> explains how stride length scales with height and speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt width.</strong> Every current ProForm except the TLS has a 20-inch belt;
            the TLS is 18 inches. Twenty inches is the common width at this price. It is narrower
            than the 22 inches on NordicTrack's Commercial series and some Sole models, which
            matters most to broad-shouldered runners and anyone who drifts on the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>User capacity.</strong> The two Pro Series machines are rated to 350 lb and the
            two Carbon Series machines to 300 lb. A rating is a ceiling rather than a comfortable
            operating point: a machine used near its limit every day works its motor, rollers and
            belt harder than one used well within it. If the heaviest user in the household is over
            about 250 lb, the Pro Series is the safer choice, and above about 300 lb our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a> covers machines built with more margin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint and height.</strong> ProForm lists a 7.75-inch step-up height for all
            four, which is low for a folding treadmill and helps anyone who finds stepping onto a
            deck awkward. The TLX, Pro 2000 and Pro 9000 all list a footprint of about 73 by 35
            inches; the TLS is smaller at about 67.5 by 31.6 inches. All four fold vertically and
            roll on wheels, though one independent review notes that the Carbon Pro 9000 is hard to
            roll over thick carpet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit-dependence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much the Machine Depends on iFIT</h2>
<p>
            Every ProForm treadmill is built around iFIT, the subscription platform ProForm shares
            with NordicTrack. iFIT supplies trainer-led classes and outdoor-route workouts, and on
            every current ProForm it can change speed and incline for you during those workouts.
            ProForm's product pages state the terms plainly: a free account and agreement to terms
            are required to activate the equipment, and a paid iFIT subscription and Wi-Fi are
            required to access the full content and features. The membership auto-renews unless
            cancelled. ProForm.com purchases included one month of membership on the day we checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On the Pro Series,</strong> iFIT runs on the built-in touchscreen. Without a
            paid membership, independent reviews describe a manual mode where you set speed and
            incline yourself, with much of the screen's value gone; streaming apps also sit behind
            the membership. The motor, deck and frame work exactly as before.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On the Carbon Series,</strong> iFIT runs on your own phone or tablet. An
            independent review of the Carbon TLX reports that the treadmill has to be unlocked
            through the app on a device before first use, even if you never subscribe, and that
            iFIT's audio plays through the device rather than the treadmill's speakers. Once set
            up, the console's buttons control speed and incline without any subscription.
          </p>
          <p>
            That has two practical consequences. First, the screen on a Pro Series machine is worth
            most while you subscribe, so if you are unsure whether you will keep paying, weight the
            deck and the user rating more heavily and the screen less. Second, a Carbon Series
            machine loses less when a membership lapses, because there is less built-in hardware
            sitting idle — but it is not a subscription-free treadmill either, because of the
            activation step. If you want a machine that is entirely independent of any platform,
            ProForm is the wrong brand, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> roundup is the better starting point.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> covers
            what the membership includes and how to think about its cost over several years, and
            our <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a> sets it against the best-known alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Warranty Structure, Read by Component</h2>
<p>
            ProForm's warranty is split by component, and reading it that way says more about the
            range than any single headline figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame: ten years across the range.</strong> Every source we checked — ProForm's
            series pages, its product pages and independent reviews — agrees on ten years for the
            frame on all four current models. It does not distinguish one machine from another.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor: two years at most.</strong> ProForm and an independent review agree on
            two years of motor cover for the Carbon Pro 2000 and Carbon Pro 9000. For the Carbon
            TLX and TLS, ProForm's series page and comparison table say two years while a badge on
            each product page says one, so confirm the term in writing before you order. Either
            way, this is the clearest difference between ProForm and the treadmills it is usually
            compared with: NordicTrack covers the motor for life on its Commercial 1750, 2450 and X
            Series, and Sole covers the frame and motor for life across its folding range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts, screen and labour: short.</strong> ProForm lists two years on parts and
            one year on labour for both series, and one year on the tablet for the Pro Series.
            Independent reviews of the Carbon Series quote one year on parts. Short labour cover
            means a technician's visit after the first year is usually at your expense, and on the
            Pro Series the screen — the most expensive single component to replace — has the
            shortest cover of all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Warranty terms vary by retailer and change between model years, so confirm them for
            the exact unit. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how to read component terms. When something does go wrong, our <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm troubleshooting guide</a> covers the resets and checks to try before calling for service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which ProForm Treadmills to Skip</h2>
<p>
            ProForm has renamed and retired machines faster than most brands, and many of the
            models that still appear in search results, review roundups and third-party listings
            are not part of the current range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Discontinued names sold as new or nearly new.</strong> The Pro 5000, the <a href="/proform-power-995i-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Power
            995i</a> and 1295i, the 505 CST, the Trainer series and the Sport series were not listed on
            ProForm's own US site on 13 October 2026, and neither was the Carbon TL, which still
            appears in a large US retailer's listings. Some of these are reasonable machines at a genuine
            clearance price, but check how old the stock is, which console and software version it
            runs, whether iFIT still fully supports it, and who honours the warranty. Our <a href="/proform-pro-5000-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Pro 5000 review</a> covers the most capable of the retired models.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An old Pro 9000 sold on an old review.</strong> Earlier machines sold under the
            Pro 9000 name were specified with a 22-inch screen and -3% decline, and those reviews
            are still widely read. The current Carbon Pro 9000 has a 16-inch screen and no
            decline. If a listing promises decline or a 22-inch screen, it is either the older
            machine or a mistake; either way, find out which before you pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Carbon TLX at Pro 2000 money.</strong> At the prices we saw, the TLX cost
            exactly what the Pro 2000 did, and the Pro 2000 adds a touchscreen and 50 lb of user
            capacity on the same deck. Buy the TLX only when it is clearly cheaper.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Any model at list price.</strong> ProForm discounted every treadmill on its site
            on the day we checked, by between $200 and $500. Wait for a sale; there is nearly always one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owning-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Setup and the First Month</h2>
<p>
            The decision does not end at checkout, and a few practical points are worth knowing
            before a ProForm arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The boxes are heavy.</strong> ProForm lists in-box weights of about 205 lb for
            the Carbon TLS and about 250 lb for the other three, in boxes roughly 78 to 84 inches long.
            Standard shipping is curbside; in-home delivery and assembly cost extra. Measure the
            route from the door to the room, including stair turns, and decide early whether to pay
            for assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plan the space in three dimensions.</strong> Add the 7.75-inch step-up height,
            the extra lift at 12% incline, your own height and a margin for the bounce of running,
            and check the total against the ceiling — particularly in a basement. Folded, all four
            stand much taller than they sit, so check the height under any shelf or window you plan
            to park one beneath.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Give it its own power.</strong> Treadmill motors draw a lot of current on
            start-up and under load, and the manuals generally specify a dedicated grounded
            circuit. Our <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cord and circuit guide</a> explains why plugging into a shared power strip causes faults that look like a failing
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Diary the iFIT renewal.</strong> A purchase from ProForm's site came with a month
            of membership on the day we checked, and it renews at the full price automatically. Note
            the date the machine arrives and decide before it renews whether you are using the
            classes enough to keep paying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the trial properly.</strong> ProForm offers a 30-day in-home trial, with a
            return fee. Run the machine at the speeds and inclines you actually plan to use in the
            first week, not a cautious ten minutes. Listen for motor or roller noise, watch whether
            the belt drifts, and time how long incline changes take, which independent reviews
            flag as slow on both Pro Series models. A fault found in the first month is a return;
            the same fault found later is a warranty claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy a Different Brand</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you do not want a subscription.</strong> Every ProForm needs an iFIT account
            to activate and a paid membership for most of its smart features. If you will never pay
            for it, a machine built without a platform is better value — see our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> roundup, or our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole roundup</a> for a brand whose apps are free.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a long motor warranty.</strong> Two years is the most ProForm offers.
            Sole covers the motor for life on its folding treadmills, and NordicTrack does on its
            better machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want steep incline or decline.</strong> No current ProForm goes beyond 12%
            or below 0%. Our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack roundup</a> covers machines that decline and <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>incline trainers</a> that reach 40%.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 300 lb, or run fast and often.</strong> The Pro
            Series is rated to 350 lb and every ProForm tops out at 12 mph on a 20-inch belt. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a> and <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a> cover machines with more margin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a ProForm</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from how quickly ProForm renames, reprices and retires its machines rather than from the machines themselves.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying from an old review of the same name</h3>
              <p>Earlier Pro 9000s had a 22-inch screen and decline; the current Carbon Pro 9000 has neither. Check the specification on ProForm’s current product page for the model you are buying, not a review written about an earlier machine.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying list price</h3>
              <p>Every ProForm treadmill was discounted by $200 to $500 on the day we checked. If a model is at full price today, it will very likely be cheaper within weeks.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing the TLX over the Pro 2000 at the same price</h3>
              <p>The two share a deck, speed and incline range, but the Pro 2000 adds a touchscreen and 50 lb of user capacity. Compare the actual sale prices on the day before choosing the cheaper-sounding series.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming a Carbon Series machine needs no iFIT at all</h3>
              <p>There is no built-in screen to subscribe through, but an independent review of the TLX reports the machine must still be activated through the iFIT app on a phone or tablet. Have a compatible device ready on delivery day.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For most buyers the <strong class="text-white">Carbon Pro 2000</strong> is the ProForm to
            buy: a full-size deck, a built-in screen and the highest user rating in the range,
            bought on discount. Walkers on a budget should look at the <strong class="text-white">Carbon TLS</strong>, and anyone who wants the largest screen
            at the <strong class="text-white">Carbon Pro 9000</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, check the specification and warranty on ProForm's current listing
            rather than an older review, and budget for iFIT as an ongoing cost — or choose a brand
            that does not need it.
          </p>
        </div>

