        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Which ProForm treadmill is best?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the Carbon Pro 2000. It has a 20 by 60-inch deck, a 10-inch touchscreen, 12 mph, 12% incline and a 350 lb user rating, and on the day we checked it cost the same as the screenless Carbon TLX. Choose the Carbon Pro 9000 for a 16-inch screen and the Carbon TLS if you mainly walk and want to spend least.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What treadmills does ProForm sell in 2026?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 13 October 2026 ProForm listed four treadmills on its US site: the Carbon Pro 2000 and Carbon Pro 9000 in the Pro Series, and the Carbon TLX and Carbon TLS in the Carbon Series. Older models such as the Pro 5000, Power 995i and 1295i, Trainer and Sport machines and the Carbon TL were not listed, though some remain at other retailers.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a ProForm treadmill without iFIT?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Partly. ProForm requires a free iFIT account to activate the machine, and a paid membership for classes, automatic speed and incline control and streaming. Without a paid membership you set speed and incline yourself, and the motor, deck and frame work as normal. An independent review of the Carbon TLX says it must be unlocked through the app on a phone or tablet first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Carbon Pro 9000 worth it over the Pro 2000?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if you will use the larger screen in most sessions or want the higher motor rating. ProForm and an independent review agree that both have a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 0 to 12% incline, a 350 lb rating and the same warranty. The 9000 adds a 16-inch screen instead of 10 inches and a 3.6 CHP motor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the ProForm Carbon TLX and TLS?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The deck, the speed and the display. ProForm and independent reviews agree that the TLX has a 20 by 60-inch belt, 12 mph and a 7-inch LCD, and the TLS an 18 by 55-inch belt, 10 mph and a 5-inch LCD. Both reach 12% incline, are rated to 300 lb, fold, and use your own device for iFIT workouts.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long is the ProForm treadmill warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              ProForm lists ten years on the frame across the range. The Pro Series has two years on the motor and parts and one year on the tablet and labour. For the Carbon Series, ProForm’s pages give the motor warranty as two years in one place and one year in another, so confirm the terms in writing for the exact unit before ordering.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

